course: dictionary
lesson: 1899
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. Colony is a good example: it can mean a group of animals, a country ruled by another country, a group of people in a new place, or a group of bacteria in a dish.
explain: «Termites are sometimes called white ants but are not ants at all; their colonies have a king as well as a queen.» Termites live together in groups, and the king and queen tell you a colony here means a group of animals living together.
explain: «Some countries grew rich partly by taking land, goods and labour from colonies.» The countries are doing the taking, so colonies must be places ruled by another country. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then swap your meaning in and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Colony means a group of animals or plants of one kind that live together, as in «a colony of ants».
fact: A colony can also be a country or area ruled by another, usually faraway, country, as in «the thirteen American colonies».
fact: A colony can be a group of people living together in a new place, far from their home country.
fact: In science, a colony can be a group of bacteria or mould growing together on a dish.
fact: A former colony is a place that was once ruled by another country and later became independent.
exercise: «Termites have a king and a queen, and their colony can be very large.» What does colony mean here? | A group of bacteria on a dish | A country ruled by another country | A group of people in a new place | *A group of animals living together
exercise: «The thirteen American colonies were ruled by Britain.» What does colony mean in this sentence? | A group of bacteria on a dish | *A country or area ruled by another, faraway country | A school club for friends | A group of ants in a nest
exercise: «Each chick has its own voice in a noisy colony.» Find and copy one word that tells you the colony is loud. | parents | chick | *noisy | voice
test: «Bacteria grew in a colony on the agar plate.» What does colony mean here? | A faraway country ruled by another | *A group of bacteria growing together | A house for ants under a tree | A group of penguins on the ice
test: Tom says colony always means a group of animals. Is he right? | No, it means a school class | Yes, only ants live in colonies | *No, it can also mean a ruled country, a group of people in a new place, or bacteria on a dish | Yes, that is its only meaning
exam: Which sentence uses colony to mean a faraway country ruled by another? | Bats roost together in a colony in the cave | *Britain had colonies in America and Africa | The bacteria grew in a colony on the dish | The ants built a new colony under the old tree
